ow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Battery: D-size Li, non-replaceable, Umin = 2.9 V, Umax = 3.7 V, lifetime 18 years\n\nSection 4: Marking and inscriptions — skip, too noisy, but could mention.\n\nPage 7:\n- Trademarks: N logo OR WILLFAR®\n- Security measures: seals\n\nPage 8: Dimensions figures — no actual values.\n\nNow, how to structure the JSON:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-26.06\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"Slovakia\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-06-12\"\n- project_number: not present — omit\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly — omit\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"\n- address_lines: [\"Bratislava / Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"] — hmm, the markdown says \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), Bratislava / Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\". I'll do address_lines: [\"Bratislava / Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"]. Actually maybe [\"Bratislava / Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\"].\n- person_responsible: \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD.\"\n- person_title: \"Director of PCB\" — hmm, \"Director of PCB\". Weird, but that's what's there. Actually \"PCB\" might be a department. Keep as given.\n- phone, email, website: not present — omit\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit\n\napplicants[]:\n- name: \"Hunan Winmeter Energy Technology Co., Ltd.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Changsha, Hunan\", \"P.R. China\"]\n\nmanufacturers[]: same — \"Applicant & Manufacturer\" — so manufacturer is the same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (the schema examples list categories; water meter fits)\n- type_designations: [\"AFlow LXC\"]\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device\" — hmm, the schema says module_designation is like \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Here it's a water meter with electronic indication device. I could put the given value. Actually per R49, module designation doesn't quite apply, but the doc states \"Module Designation: Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device\". I'll record it as given.\n- description: could add a brief description of the water meter. \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device\" for cold potable water and hot water per R49. Maybe description: \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device for cold potable water and hot water (OIML R 49)\". Keep modest.\n\nmodel_family: Do we have multiple models? The certificate covers DN15–DN20 and DN25–DN50 sizes (from security measures and figures). There are dimensional drawings for DN15-DN20 and DN25-DN50 groups. This suggests size variants DN15, DN20, DN25, DN50. But no numeric values per size given in this fragment (dimensions figure has no values transcribed). Should I include model_family with models DN15, DN20, DN25, DN50? The cert mentions \"DN15-DN50\" (Fig. 3a) and \"DN15-DN20\" (Fig. 3b). The type designation \"AFlow LXC\" covers nominal diameters DN15 to DN50. I think including a model_family with models [\"DN15\", \"DN20\", \"DN25\", \"DN50\"] is reasonable. But careful about fabricating — DN25–DN50 range shown as group. The security section says DN15-DN50 for Fig 3a seals, DN15-DN20 for Fig 3b. So the family includes DN15, DN20, DN25, ..., DN50? The drawings show \"DN15–DN20\" and \"DN25–DN50\" groups. Common water meter sizes: DN15, 20, 25, 32, 40, 50. But the fragment doesn't list 32/40 explicitly. Hmm. Safer: include model_family with models defined by DN groups? That seems forced. Alternatively, note in characteristics a config-level? Actually the schema says include model_family ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. It does cover multiple DN sizes. I could list models: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN50 based on what's explicitly written? DN15-DN50 is a range; intermediate sizes unknown but likely. I think it's safer to include model_family with family_name \"AFlow LXC\" and models for the two documented groups? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe better: include models as DN15–DN20 group and DN25–DN50 group? That's odd for model_id. Let me think about what's most faithful: The cert states the water meters AFlow LXC come in sizes DN15 to DN50. I'll create model_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"AFlow LXC\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-DN20\", \"label\": \"Dimension group DN15–DN20\", \"attributes\": {\"security\": \"four plastic seals for lower plastic cover\"}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN25-DN50\", \"label\": \"Dimension group DN25–DN50\", \"attributes\": {}}\n  ]\n}\nHmm, but the security seals: Fig 3a applies to DN15-DN50 (upper cover seals), Fig 3b applies to DN15-DN20 (lower plastic cover seals). That's a config-level distinction. This is getting noisy.\n\nAlternative: skip model_family since there's no attribute matrix per model in this fragment. The rule says include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. The cert does cover DN15–DN50. But since the fragment lacks per-model characteristics, maybe minimal model_family with the size variants is fine. I'll include it with the two documented drawing groups? Hmm, risk of fabrication. The text explicitly mentions DN15-DN50 and DN15-DN20 as ranges. I think listing models as [\"DN15-DN50\"] is not a model, it's a range.\n\nLet me consider: characteristics could include a config_level for security measures? Probably too noisy. Rules say don't fabricate; also structured extraction focuses on measurable characteristics.\n\nDecision: include model_family with family_name \"AFlow LXC\" and models [{\"model_id\": \"DN15\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN20\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN25\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"}]? That fabricates intermediate sizes absent (DN32, DN40) and asserts endpoints only. Hmm.\n\nActually, the safest: omit model_family because the fragment doesn't enumerate discrete models. But the instruction #4 says detect matrix; there's no matrix here. I'll include a light model_family noting the size range via certified_type.description instead: \"...available in sizes DN15 to DN50\".\n\nHmm, but the model_family with the two groups is actually documented: Fig 2 shows dimensions for DN15–DN20 and DN25–DN50 groups. Security: Fig 3a DN15-DN50, Fig 3b DN15-DN20. I could use config_level for the security measure axis? Too noisy.\n\nI'll go with model_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"AFlow LXC\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-DN20\", \"label\": \"Dimension group DN15–DN20\", \"attributes\": {\"security\": \"four plastic seals for lower plastic cover\"}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN25-DN50\", \"label\": \"Dimension group DN25–DN50\", \"attributes\": {}}\n  ]\n}\nHmm, but the security seals: Fig 3a applies to DN15-DN50 (upper cover seals), Fig 3b applies to DN15-DN20 (lower plastic cover seals). That's a config-level distinction. This is getting noisy.\n\nActually, I think I'll include it as a config axis: attribute \"plastic_seals_on_lower_cover\", axis \"nominal_diameter_group\". Eh.\n\nLet me reconsider — keep it simpler and faithful:\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: value 16, unit \"bar\"\n- pressure_loss: value 0.4, unit \"bar\"\n- pressure_loss_class: value \"Δp40\" — as string \"Δp40\". Canonical label: pressure_loss_class. Value \"Δp 40\" → \"Δp40\" (string). Hmm, the Δp class is \"Δp 40\". Value string: \"Δp40\". Fine.\n- protection_class: \"IP68\"\n- maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone: value: 2, unit \"%\"? It's ±2% at Θ ≤ 30°C and ±3% at Θ > 30°C. That's config-level (temperature condition). So config_level:\n  - attribute: maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone, axis: water_temperature, values: [{condition: \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"Θ > 30 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]\n  - Hmm, values are ±2 i.e. ±2%. The value could be 2 with a note of ±? I'll use value 2 and unit \"%\", maybe add attribute label \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\". Could include value as 2 (magnitude). Or \"±2\"? Rules say numbers not strings; ±2 → maybe value: -2 to 2? No — MPE is ±2%. I'll record value: 2, unit \"%\", and perhaps a \"mpe_sign\": \"±\"? Too noisy. I'll just do value 2 and 3 in config_level, attribute name maximum_permitted_error (upper zone). Alternatively keep at type_level as two separate entries? The rule 5 says when attributes vary by configuration (static vs dynamic etc.), use config_level. Temperature condition fits config_level. Good.\n- maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone: value 5, unit \"%\" (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2). Could be config_level with axis flow_zone? Could do config_level axis \"flow zone\": condition \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\" → 2 (≤30°C)... but there are two axes (zone and temperature). Keep simpler:\n  - config_level entry 1: attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\", axis \"water_temperature\", values [{condition \"≤30°C\", value 2}, {condition \">30°C\", value 3}]\n  - type_level: maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone: 5 \"%\"\n- calculator_capacity_normal_resolution: 999999.999, unit \"m³\"\n- calculator_capacity_high_resolution: 9999.999999, unit \"m³\"\n- scale_interval_normal_resolution: 0.001, unit \"m³\"\n- scale_interval_high_resolution: 0.000001, unit \"m³\"\n- accuracy_class: 2 (dimensionless, unit null)\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min: -25, max: 70}, unit \"°C\"\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E1/E2\"\n- environmental_class_climatic_mechanical: \"B/O\" — label: \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class\": \"B/O\". Hmm, per R49, \"B\" is climatic (condensing), \"O\" is mechanical? Actually in R49, climatic class B, mechanical class O... The table says \"Climatic/mechanical environmental conditions: B/O\". Label: climatic_mechanical_environmental_conditions, value \"B/O\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery info: multiple attributes:\n  - battery_type: \"D-size lithium (Li), non-replaceable\" — string. Or split: battery_size \"D\", battery_chemistry \"Li\", battery_replaceable false.\n  - battery_nominal_voltage? We have Umin = 2.9 V, Umax = 3.7 V. So:\n    - battery_minimum_voltage: 2.9, unit \"V\"\n    - battery_maximum_voltage: 3.7, unit \"V\"\n  - battery_lifetime: 18, unit \"years\"\n\nNote the original markdown used commas as decimals: \"0,40\" → 0.40; \"999999,999\" → 999999.999; \"0,001\" → 0.001; \"0,000001\" → 0.000001; \"2,9\" → 2.9; \"3,7\" → 3.7. Yes, fix decimals.\n\nmatrix_tables: none really — the tech characteristics table is a simple two-column list, captured in type_level. No multi-dimensional matrix.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"2026/ER057/SK1\", date: \"2026-06-12\", pages: 17, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- documentation file: {id: \"Technical documentation file Hunan Winmeter_AFlow LX_00\", date: \"2026-06-12\", pages: 34, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2026-06-12\", changes: \"Certificate first issued\"}\n\nfootnotes: none present.\n\nComponents: none named separately (the meter is a single instrument). Omit.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\" (certificate scheme A), accuracy_classes: [2]. Accuracy class for water meters is 2. Include accuracy_classes: [2]. Hmm — R49 uses accuracy class 1 and 2; here class 2. Good.\n\ncertificate.project_number: not present. page_total: 12. member_state: \"Slovakia\". date_issued: \"2026-06-12\". oiml_issuer_id: not given.\n\nissuing_authority name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\". address_lines: [\"Bratislava / Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"]. Actually the summary line: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), Bratislava / Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\". I'll use address_lines: [\"Bratislava / Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"].\n\nperson_responsible: \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD.\"; person_title: \"Director of PCB\". Hmm \"Director of PCB\" — odd; keep as-is since can't verify.\n\napplicants: Hunan Winmeter Energy Technology Co., Ltd., address_lines [\"Changsha, Hunan\", \"P.R. China\"].\n\nmanufacturers: same entity.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AFlow LXC\"]\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device\"\n- description: \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device for cold potable water and hot water, sizes DN15 to DN50; trademarks: N logo or WILLFAR®.\"\n\nWait — should description mention trademarks? Fine.\n\nmodel_family — final decision: The fragment explicitly indicates two dimensional/ construction groups (DN15–DN20, DN25–DN50) via Fig. 2 and security differences. I'll include:\n\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": \"AFlow LXC\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-DN20\", \"label\": \"Dimensions per Fig. 2, DN15–DN20 group\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN25-DN50\", \"label\": \"Dimensions per Fig. 2, DN25–DN50 group\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm. Is this over-engineering? The schema says include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. Cert covers DN range → yes multiple variants. I think including with these two documented groups is defensible and faithful. Alternatively model_ids \"DN15\", \"DN20\", \"DN25\", \"DN50\"? No — intermediate sizes unknown. I'll go with the two groups. Actually, hmm, one more consideration: the security section says Fig 3a applies to DN15-DN50 and Fig 3b to DN15-DN20. I could add attributes: security config. I'll keep models minimal without attributes, and not add config_level for seals (too noisy, but rules say don't fabricate — including seals isn't fabrication, but it's a security description not a metrological characteristic; keep in description? No, description is for the type. I'll leave security out or maybe include in description briefly.)\n\nActually maybe I should include the security info in description: \"Sealed with four plastic lead seals and two wire lead seals (upper cover, DN15–DN50) and four plastic seals (lower plastic cover, DN15–DN20).\" That preserves info without contorting schema. Good.\n\nConfig-level for MPE by temperature — as decided.\n\nAlso display codes (page 4) — skip; test mode precision values like 0.000001 m³? The test mode display precision: indicated volume 0,000001 m³; instantaneous flow 0,000001 m³/h; water temp 0,1 °C. These are display precisions in test mode. Could add as characteristics: test_mode_display_resolution_volume: 0.000001 m³, etc. Hmm, they are legit characteristics. But the page is a GLM summary (\"I can see this is page 4... here's a summary\"). The data is present in the markdown. I could include:\n- test_mode_scale_interval_volume? Eh. To be safe and useful, I'll include a few: \n  - \"test_mode_volume_display_resolution\": 0.000001 m³\n  - \"test_mode_flow_rate_display_resolution\": 0.000001 m³/h\n  - \"test_mode_temperature_display_resolution\": 0.1 °C\n\nHmm — risk of noise. The schema doesn't forbid extra attributes. I think including them adds fidelity. But also there's \"scale interval (high resolution)\" 0.000001 m³ which matches the test mode precision. I'll include the three test mode display resolutions. Actually, let me reconsider: the instruction says extract characteristics present. These are present. I'll include them.\n\nHmm, actually maybe keep it leaner. The main characteristics table is the core. The display codes are operational. I'll include the test mode display precisions since they are numeric characteristics. Fine.\n\nSoftware version info (page 4: code 011 software version etc.) — no actual version values, just codes. Skip.\n\nBattery: \"D-size Li, non-replaceable\" — I'll do:\n- \"battery\": could be a components entry? The battery is a component of the meter. Rule 6: components[] for sub-assemblies — battery could be a component. But components have type_designations; battery has no type designation. Better to keep battery characteristics at type_level:\n  - battery_chemistry: \"Li\" (lithium)\n  - battery_size: \"D\"\n  - battery_replaceable: false (non-replaceable) — boolean; value must be number|range|string per shape. Rules say value: <number | {min,max} | string>. Boolean not allowed strictly. Use string \"non-replaceable\". I'll do battery_type: \"D-size lithium, non-replaceable\".\n  - battery_minimum_voltage: 2.9 V\n  - battery_maximum_voltage: 3.7 V\n  - battery_lifetime: 18 years\n\nLabels: canonical snake_case: \"battery_nominal_voltage_min\"? I'll use battery_minimum_voltage / battery_maximum_voltage. Or battery_voltage with value {min:2.9, max:3.7}? The source says Umin=2.9 V, Umax=3.7 V. Range representation: battery_voltage: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}. Hmm, but that conflates operating voltage range. Actually Umin/Umax of battery = voltage range. Range form is compact and valid. I'll do:\n- \"battery_voltage\": {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- \"battery_type\": \"D-size Li, non-replaceable\"\n- \"battery_lifetime\": 18, unit \"years\" — unit_symbol \"years\" (or \"a\"). Use \"years\".\n\nPressure loss: \"0,40 / Δp 40\" → pressure_loss 0.4 bar; pressure_loss_class \"Δp40\".\n\nMAP: maximum_admissible_pressure: 16 bar. Also marking includes MAP — skip.\n\nProtection class: IP68 → protection_class: \"IP68\".\n\nTemperature class (T) in markings — value not given; skip.\n\nElectromagnetic class \"E1/E2\" → string.\n\nClimatic class −25 to +70 °C → {min: -25, max: 70} °C.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity class U0D0 → \"U0D0\".\n\nAccuracy class 2 → value 2. And recommendation.accuracy_classes [2].\n\nMPE: careful with the ± sign. Upper zone ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C). I'll record values 2 and 3 with unit \"%\", attribute names \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\" / \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone\". Should I encode \"±\"? The value as number loses the ±. Maybe attribute names convey MPE. Fine.\n\nNow test_reports roles:\n- 2026/ER057/SK1 → \"type evaluation report\" → role \"type_evaluation_report\".\n- documentation file → \"documentation_file\", id: \"Technical documentation file Hunan Winmeter_AFlow LX_00\". The id field — use the file name: \"Hunan Winmeter_AFlow LX_00\"? The doc says: documentation file name: „Technical documentation file Hunan Winmeter_AFlow LX_00\". I'll set id: \"Technical documentation file Hunan Winmeter_AFlow LX_00\".\n\nDates: \"12th June 2026\" → \"2026-06-12\".\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2026-06-12\".\n\nscheme: \"A\".\n\nmember_state: \"Slovakia\".\n\npage_total: 12.\n\nNumber: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-26.06\".\n\nFootnotes: none. matrix_tables: none — omit.\n\nmodel_family: include as decided. Actually, let me reconsider once more whether to include model_family. Rule 4: \"Many OIML certs cover a FAMILY of instrument models, where attributes vary per model. Detect this and put varying attributes in model_level.\" Here no attributes vary per model in this fragment. The cert covers DN15–DN50. Including model_family models without model_level characteristics is okay (\"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\"). I'll include with the two documented groups as model_ids \"DN15-DN20\" and \"DN25-DN50\". Hmm, but is that inventing a grouping? Fig 2 explicitly shows \"DN15–DN20\" and \"DN25–DN50\" drawings — that's a documented grouping. OK.\n\nActually — wait. Maybe better to list individual sizes DN15, DN20, DN25, DN50 since Fig 3b mentions DN15-DN20 for lower cover seals, implying sizes DN15 and DN20; and Fig 3a DN15-DN50. The two-group grouping comes from Fig 2. I'll stick with groups; less fabrication.\n\ndescription: \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device for cold potable water, sizes DN15 to DN50. Trademarks: N logo or WILLFAR®. Security: four plastic lead seals and two lead seals with wire (upper cover to lower part, DN15–DN50); four plastic seals on lower plastic cover (DN15–DN20).\" Good — preserves info from pages 7.\n\nType-level characteristics labels:\n- \"maximum_admissible_pressure\"\n- \"pressure_loss\"\n- \"pressure_loss_class\"\n- \"protection_class\"\n- \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\" (via config_level actually)\n- \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone\"\n- \"calculator_capacity_normal_resolution\" → maybe \"calculator_capacity\" with config axis resolution? Two variants (normal/high) — that